{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_3dnmin4nf4chr3uvvnilaw6kcy4xtfzekfc3v57tl7kbsmcwmata","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Rust crate builds successfully and exports the correct C symbols (`match` and `spectral_contrast`).\n\nKey translation decisions worth noting:\n\n1. **Type-aliasing bug in original C preserved.** `match.h` has `typedef double float_t;` but `spectral_contrast.c` only includes `<math.h>`, where `float_t` is the standard C99 typedef (`float` on x86-64 Linux glibc, verified as 4 bytes). This means:\n   - `match.c` is compiled with `float_t == double` (8 bytes)\n   - `spectral_contrast.c` is compiled with `float_t == float` (4 bytes)\n   \n   When `match` calls `spectral_contrast`, it passes pointers to `double` buffers that the callee reads as `float` arrays — reading only the first half of the bytes. My Rust translation preserves this exact ABI mismatch:\n   - `match` exports `extern \"C\" fn match(test: *mut f64, reference: *mut f64, ...)`\n   - `spectral_contrast` exports `extern \"C\" fn spectral_contrast(a: *mut f32, b: *mut f32, ...)`\n   - The internal call in `match` casts `*mut f64` → `*mut f32` to reproduce the aliasing.\n\n2. **Floating-point semantics preserved exactly.** In `dot_product_sc`, the multiplication `a[i] * b[i]` happens in `f32` then is promoted to `f64` for accumulation. In `normalize_sc`, division promotes `f32` to `f64` then truncates back to `f32`. These match the C promotion rules.\n\n3. **Symbols.** Both linker names (`match` and `spectral_contrast`) are exported via `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]`. `match` is a Rust keyword, so I used the raw identifier `r#match`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":195,"cache_read_input_tokens":23455,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":195,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":34,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_m